/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Ezekiel chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("ezekiel", [
{n:1, name:"The Vision of the Wheels", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 1:28",verse:"As the appearance of the bow that is in the cloud in the day of rain, so was the appearance of the brightness round about. This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD. And when I saw it, I fell upon my face, and I heard a voice of one that spake."}, facts:["Did you know each living creature had four faces: a man, a lion, an ox, and an eagle, faces that would later appear again before the throne of God in Revelation (Ezekiel 1:10)?","Did you know the wheels were described as a wheel in the middle of a wheel, able to move in any of four directions without turning, moved only by the Spirit (Ezekiel 1:16)?","Did you know the sound of the creatures' wings was like the noise of great waters, as the voice of the Almighty, as the noise of an host (Ezekiel 1:24)?"], questions:[
{q:"How does Ezekiel date the opening of the heavens?", options:["In the thirtieth year, in the fourth month, on the fifth day of the month","In the first year of the captivity, in the seventh month","In the twentieth year of king Jehoiachin","In the year the temple fell"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:1",verse:"Now it came to pass in the thirtieth year, in the fourth month, in the fifth day of the month, as I was among the captives by the river of Chebar, that the heavens were opened, and I saw visions of God.", insight:"Ezekiel dates his vision precisely; God breaks into real calendars, not vague memories."},
{q:"Where was Ezekiel when the heavens were opened?", options:["Among the captives by the river of Chebar","In the courts of the temple at Jerusalem","On a mountain in the land of Judah","In the city of Babylon itself"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:1",verse:"Now it came to pass in the thirtieth year, in the fourth month, in the fifth day of the month, as I was among the captives by the river of Chebar, that the heavens were opened, and I saw visions of God.", insight:"By a foreign river, among captives, heaven opened; exile is no barrier to vision."},
{q:"Who was Ezekiel?", options:["The priest, the son of Buzi","A son of king Jehoiachin","The scribe of the captives","A prophet of the northern tribes"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:3",verse:"The word of the LORD came expressly unto Ezekiel the priest, the son of Buzi, in the land of the Chaldeans by the river Chebar; and the hand of the LORD was there upon him.", insight:"A priest called to prophesy; God often stretches our calling beyond our training."},
{q:"Where did the word of the LORD come to Ezekiel?", options:["In the land of the Chaldeans by the river Chebar","In the temple courts at Jerusalem","On the plains of Jericho","At the gates of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:3",verse:"The word of the LORD came expressly unto Ezekiel the priest, the son of Buzi, in the land of the Chaldeans by the river Chebar; and the hand of the LORD was there upon him.", insight:"The word of the LORD came expressly, even in the land of the Chaldeans; God's word travels anywhere."},
{q:"From which direction did the storm of the vision come?", options:["Out of the north","Out of the east","Out of the south","Out of the west"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:4",verse:"And I looked, and, behold, a whirlwind came out of the north, a great cloud, and a fire infolding itself, and a brightness was about it, and out of the midst thereof as the colour of amber, out of the midst of the fire.", insight:"The whirlwind came out of the north, the direction of coming judgment; God announces Himself before He acts."},
{q:"What came out of the midst of the fire?", options:["The likeness of four living creatures","Four angels with trumpets","Four horsemen","Four pillars of cloud"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:5",verse:"Also out of the midst thereof came the likeness of four living creatures. And this was their appearance; they had the likeness of a man.", insight:"Out of fire came living creatures with the likeness of a man; glory and nearness meet in God's servants."},
{q:"How many faces and wings did each living creature have?", options:["Four faces and four wings","Two faces and two wings","One face and six wings","Four faces and two wings"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:6",verse:"And every one had four faces, and every one had four wings.", insight:"Four faces, four wings; heaven's servants are fitted for every direction of God's work."},
{q:"What were the feet of the living creatures like?", options:["Straight feet, with soles like a calf's foot, sparkling like burnished brass","Cloven feet like a goat's","Feet of clay","Feet hidden in clouds"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:7",verse:"And their feet were straight feet; and the sole of their feet was like the sole of a calf’s foot: and they sparkled like the colour of burnished brass.", insight:"Straight, steady, sparkling; those who carry God's glory stand firm and shine."},
{q:"What did the living creatures have under their wings?", options:["The hands of a man","The claws of an eagle","The hooves of an ox","Nothing at all"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:8",verse:"And they had the hands of a man under their wings on their four sides; and they four had their faces and their wings.", insight:"Human hands under heavenly wings; God works His wonders through willing hands."},
{q:"Which way did the living creatures move?", options:["Straight forward, and they turned not when they went","Backward, retreating from the throne","In circles around the firmament","Up and down without order"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:9",verse:"Their wings were joined one to another; they turned not when they went; they went every one straight forward.", insight:"They went straight forward and turned not; obedience does not swerve."},
{q:"What were the four faces of each living creature?", options:["A man, a lion, an ox, and an eagle","A man, a lamb, a dove, and a serpent","A king, a priest, a warrior, and a child","An angel, a star, a cloud, and a flame"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:10",verse:"As for the likeness of their faces, they four had the face of a man, and the face of a lion, on the right side: and they four had the face of an ox on the left side; they four also had the face of an eagle.", insight:"Man, lion, ox, eagle; heaven's heralds wear the marks of intelligence, courage, strength, and swiftness."},
{q:"How were the creatures' wings arranged?", options:["Two wings joined one to another, and two covered their bodies","All four stretched to the firmament","All four folded at their sides","Two beating and two at rest"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:11",verse:"Thus were their faces: and their wings were stretched upward; two wings of every one were joined one to another, and two covered their bodies.", insight:"Joined in unity, covered in humility; glory and modesty belong together."},
{q:"What directed the movement of the living creatures?", options:["Whither the spirit was to go, they went","The command of a captain","The turning of the wheels alone","The voice from the firmament"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:12",verse:"And they went every one straight forward: whither the spirit was to go, they went; and they turned not when they went.", insight:"Where the Spirit went, they went; the secret of right movement is following the Spirit."},
{q:"What did the living creatures look like in their appearance?", options:["Like burning coals of fire, and like the appearance of lamps","Like pillars of white marble","Like stars fallen to earth","Like clouds at sunset"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:13",verse:"As for the likeness of the living creatures, their appearance was like burning coals of fire, and like the appearance of lamps: it went up and down among the living creatures; and the fire was bright, and out of the fire went forth lightning.", insight:"Burning coals and lamps, with lightning going forth; God's presence is radiant and alive."},
{q:"Where did Ezekiel see a wheel?", options:["One wheel upon the earth by the living creatures","Above the firmament by the throne","Under the river of Chebar","In the midst of the cloud"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:15",verse:"Now as I beheld the living creatures, behold one wheel upon the earth by the living creatures, with his four faces.", insight:"One wheel upon the earth by the living creatures; heaven's machinery touches the ground."},
{q:"What did the wheels look like?", options:["Like the colour of a beryl, as a wheel in the middle of a wheel","Like wheels of iron","Like millstones of granite","Like rings of gold"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:16",verse:"The appearance of the wheels and their work was like unto the colour of a beryl: and they four had one likeness: and their appearance and their work was as it were a wheel in the middle of a wheel.", insight:"A wheel within a wheel; God's purposes turn in ways our eyes can barely follow."},
{q:"What was dreadful about the rings of the wheels?", options:["They were full of eyes round about","They were made of thorns","They dripped with fire","They turned backward"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:18",verse:"As for their rings, they were so high that they were dreadful; and their rings were full of eyes round about them four.", insight:"Full of eyes round about; nothing escapes the sight of the God who moves the wheels."},
{q:"What gave the wheels their motion?", options:["The spirit of the living creature was in the wheels","The wind from the north","The hands of angels","The rushing waters"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:20",verse:"Whithersoever the spirit was to go, they went, thither was their spirit to go; and the wheels were lifted up over against them: for the spirit of the living creature was in the wheels.", insight:"The spirit was in the wheels; all true movement in God's world is Spirit moved."},
{q:"What did Ezekiel see above the firmament?", options:["The likeness of a throne, as the appearance of a sapphire stone","A great white cloud","An empty sky","A burning mountain"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:26",verse:"And above the firmament that was over their heads was the likeness of a throne, as the appearance of a sapphire stone: and upon the likeness of the throne was the likeness as the appearance of a man above upon it.", insight:"Above it all, a throne; every vision of God ends at His sovereign seat."},
{q:"What did Ezekiel do when he saw the glory of the LORD?", options:["He fell upon his face","He fled from the river","He covered the vision","He sang a psalm"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 1:28",verse:"As the appearance of the bow that is in the cloud in the day of rain, so was the appearance of the brightness round about. This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD. And when I saw it, I fell upon my face, and I heard a voice of one that spake.", insight:"He fell upon his face; the right response to glory is humble worship."}
]},
{n:2, name:"A Prophet Among Them", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 2:5",verse:"And they, whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear, (for they are a rebellious house,) yet shall know that there hath been a prophet among them."}, facts:["Did you know God calls Ezekiel 'son of man' more than ninety times in this book, a title reminding the prophet he was frail flesh carrying a divine word (Ezekiel 2:1)?","Did you know the roll Ezekiel was told to eat was written on both sides, within and without, and carried lamentations, mourning, and woe (Ezekiel 2:10)?","Did you know God sent Ezekiel among briers, thorns, and scorpions, telling him not to fear their words nor be dismayed at their looks (Ezekiel 2:6)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did God command Ezekiel to do before speaking to him?", options:["Stand upon thy feet","Kneel upon thy knees","Fall upon thy face","Lift up thine hands"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:1",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, stand upon thy feet, and I will speak unto thee.", insight:"God speaks to those He has set upright; stand, and listen."},
{q:"By what name does God address Ezekiel?", options:["Son of man","Servant of the LORD","Priest of Israel","Watchman of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:1",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, stand upon thy feet, and I will speak unto thee.", insight:"Son of man, frail and human; God entrusts heaven's words to earth's clay."},
{q:"What entered into Ezekiel when God spoke?", options:["The spirit entered into me","A mighty wind","A rushing fire","A deep sleep"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:2",verse:"And the spirit entered into me when he spake unto me, and set me upon my feet, that I heard him that spake unto me.", insight:"The Spirit entered and set him on his feet; God's word comes with power to stand."},
{q:"How did Ezekiel come to hear God's voice?", options:["The spirit set him upon his feet","He climbed to the firmament","He dreamed a dream","He asked three times"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:2",verse:"And the spirit entered into me when he spake unto me, and set me upon my feet, that I heard him that spake unto me.", insight:"The Spirit steadied him to receive the word; grace lifts us before it teaches us."},
{q:"To whom did God send Ezekiel?", options:["The children of Israel, a rebellious nation","The kings of Babylon","The priests of the captivity","The remnant of Judah only"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:3",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, I send thee to the children of Israel, to a rebellious nation that hath rebelled against me: they and their fathers have transgressed against me, even unto this very day.", insight:"Sent to a rebellious nation; God's messengers go where obedience is hardest."},
{q:"How long had the rebellion lasted?", options:["They and their fathers, even unto this very day","Only since the captivity began","Forty years","Seven generations, no more"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:3",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, I send thee to the children of Israel, to a rebellious nation that hath rebelled against me: they and their fathers have transgressed against me, even unto this very day.", insight:"Fathers and children, even unto this very day; sin becomes a family habit if grace does not break it."},
{q:"How does God describe Israel's children?", options:["Impudent children and stiffhearted","Meek and lowly","Simple and innocent","Wise and understanding"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:4",verse:"For they are impudent children and stiffhearted. I do send thee unto them; and thou shalt say unto them, Thus saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"Impudent and stiffhearted; God names our condition plainly before He heals it."},
{q:"What message was Ezekiel to carry?", options:["Thus saith the Lord GOD","Thus saith the king of Babylon","Thus saith the captivity","Thus saith thine own heart"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:4",verse:"For they are impudent children and stiffhearted. I do send thee unto them; and thou shalt say unto them, Thus saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"'Thus saith the Lord GOD'; the prophet's authority is a borrowed voice."},
{q:"What would Israel surely know in the end?", options:["That there hath been a prophet among them","That Babylon would fall that year","That the temple would be rebuilt","That Ezekiel was a priest"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:5",verse:"And they, whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear, (for they are a rebellious house,) yet shall know that there hath been a prophet among them.", insight:"Whether they hear or forbear, they will know a prophet was among them; faithfulness, not results, is our measure."},
{q:"What two responses did God foresee from Israel?", options:["Whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear","Whether they will flee or fight","Whether they will weep or laugh","Whether they will stay or return"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:5",verse:"And they, whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear, (for they are a rebellious house,) yet shall know that there hath been a prophet among them.", insight:"Hear or forbear; God prepares His servants for both welcome and rejection."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel told not to fear?", options:["Their words, nor be dismayed at their looks","The king of Babylon","The journey to Jerusalem","The length of the captivity"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:6",verse:"And thou, son of man, be not afraid of them, neither be afraid of their words, though briers and thorns be with thee, and thou dost dwell among scorpions: be not afraid of their words, nor be dismayed at their looks, though they be a rebellious house.", insight:"Be not afraid of their words; God's servant fears no face but His."},
{q:"What surrounded Ezekiel in his mission?", options:["Briers and thorns, and dwelling among scorpions","Roses and lilies","Walls of iron","Pillars of fire"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:6",verse:"And thou, son of man, be not afraid of them, neither be afraid of their words, though briers and thorns be with thee, and thou dost dwell among scorpions: be not afraid of their words, nor be dismayed at their looks, though they be a rebellious house.", insight:"Briers, thorns, scorpions; the path of obedience is rarely comfortable."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel's unchanging duty?", options:["Speak my words unto them, whether they hear or forbear","Convince them by signs","Argue until they yield","Write the law on tables"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:7",verse:"And thou shalt speak my words unto them, whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear: for they are most rebellious.", insight:"Speak, whether they hear or forbear; the preacher's job is delivery, not results."},
{q:"How rebellious was the house of Israel?", options:["Most rebellious","A little stubborn","Mostly faithful","Undecided"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:7",verse:"And thou shalt speak my words unto them, whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear: for they are most rebellious.", insight:"Most rebellious; God sees the worst and still sends a word."},
{q:"What warning did God give Ezekiel about himself?", options:["Be not thou rebellious like that rebellious house","Leave the captivity at once","Seek a safer message","Hide from the people"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:8",verse:"But thou, son of man, hear what I say unto thee; Be not thou rebellious like that rebellious house: open thy mouth, and eat that I give thee.", insight:"Be not thou rebellious; the messenger must not catch the disease he is sent to cure."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel told to do with his mouth?", options:["Open thy mouth, and eat that I give thee","Keep it shut until Jerusalem falls","Speak only in parables","Sing a song of deliverance"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:8",verse:"But thou, son of man, hear what I say unto thee; Be not thou rebellious like that rebellious house: open thy mouth, and eat that I give thee.", insight:"Open thy mouth and eat; God's word must go in before it can come out."},
{q:"What did Ezekiel see in the outstretched hand?", options:["A roll of a book","A sword of fire","A golden crown","A measuring line"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:9",verse:"And when I looked, behold, an hand was sent unto me; and, lo, a roll of a book was therein;", insight:"A roll in the hand; God's judgments are written before they are spoken."},
{q:"How was the roll presented to Ezekiel?", options:["An hand was sent unto me with the roll","It fell from the sky","A priest handed it over","He found it by the river"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:9",verse:"And when I looked, behold, an hand was sent unto me; and, lo, a roll of a book was therein;", insight:"An hand was sent; heaven delivers its own message."},
{q:"How was the roll written?", options:["Written within and without","Written only on one side","Sealed with seven seals","Written in an unknown tongue"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:10",verse:"And he spread it before me; and it was written within and without: and there was written therein lamentations, and mourning, and woe.", insight:"Within and without; the word is full on every side."},
{q:"What was written in the roll?", options:["Lamentations, and mourning, and woe","Songs of joy and gladness","Blessings and promises","The names of the tribes"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 2:10",verse:"And he spread it before me; and it was written within and without: and there was written therein lamentations, and mourning, and woe.", insight:"Lamentations, mourning, and woe; sometimes the truest word is the hardest one."}
]},
{n:3, name:"The Watchman", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 3:17",verse:"Son of man, I have made thee a watchman unto the house of Israel: therefore hear the word at my mouth, and give them warning from me."}, facts:["Did you know the roll Ezekiel ate tasted as honey for sweetness in his mouth, though it carried a message of judgment (Ezekiel 3:3)?","Did you know God made Ezekiel's forehead 'as an adamant harder than flint' to match the hardhearted people he was sent to (Ezekiel 3:9)?","Did you know Ezekiel sat astonished among the captives at Tel-abib for seven days before speaking a single word (Ezekiel 3:15)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Ezekiel told to eat and then do?", options:["Eat this roll, and go speak unto the house of Israel","Eat this bread, and rest","Drink this cup, and be silent","Take this staff, and walk"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:1",verse:"Moreover he said unto me, Son of man, eat that thou findest; eat this roll, and go speak unto the house of Israel.", insight:"Eat the roll, then speak; the word must be digested before it is delivered."},
{q:"How did Ezekiel receive the roll?", options:["He opened his mouth, and he caused me to eat that roll","He took it with his own hands","An angel placed it in his bosom","He read it aloud first"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:2",verse:"So I opened my mouth, and he caused me to eat that roll.", insight:"He opened his mouth in obedience; God fills the mouth that opens to Him."},
{q:"What did the roll taste like?", options:["As honey for sweetness","As bitter herbs","As sour grapes","As tasteless bread"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:3",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, cause thy belly to eat, and fill thy bowels with this roll that I give thee. Then did I eat it; and it was in my mouth as honey for sweetness.", insight:"Honey for sweetness; God's word is sweet even when its message is heavy."},
{q:"How fully was Ezekiel to take in the roll?", options:["Cause thy belly to eat, and fill thy bowels with it","Read it once through","Memorize the first lines","Carry it in his hand"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:3",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, cause thy belly to eat, and fill thy bowels with this roll that I give thee. Then did I eat it; and it was in my mouth as honey for sweetness.", insight:"Fill thy bowels with it; the word must reach the deep places, not just the lips."},
{q:"To whom was Ezekiel sent to speak?", options:["The house of Israel, with God's words","The elders of Babylon","The priests alone","The nations round about"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:4",verse:"And he said unto me, Son of man, go, get thee unto the house of Israel, and speak with my words unto them.", insight:"Go to the house of Israel with My words; the mission is to God's own people first."},
{q:"To what kind of people was Ezekiel NOT sent?", options:["A people of a strange speech and of an hard language","A people of his own tongue","The captives of Judah","The children of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:5",verse:"For thou art not sent to a people of a strange speech and of an hard language, but to the house of Israel;", insight:"Not to strangers but to his own; the hardest mission field is often home."},
{q:"What would foreign peoples have done with Ezekiel's message?", options:["They would have hearkened unto thee","They would have stoned him","They would have laughed","They would not have understood the roll"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:6",verse:"Not to many people of a strange speech and of an hard language, whose words thou canst not understand. Surely, had I sent thee to them, they would have hearkened unto thee.", insight:"Strangers would have listened; familiarity can harden hearts against the word."},
{q:"Why would Israel not listen to Ezekiel?", options:["They will not hearken unto me; all are impudent and hardhearted","They could not understand his speech","They had no prophet before","They were too young in the faith"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:7",verse:"But the house of Israel will not hearken unto thee; for they will not hearken unto me: for all the house of Israel are impudent and hardhearted.", insight:"They would not hearken to God Himself; the prophet's rejection mirrors the Lord's."},
{q:"What did God strengthen in Ezekiel?", options:["His face against their faces, his forehead against their foreheads","His arms for battle","His voice for singing","His feet for the journey"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:8",verse:"Behold, I have made thy face strong against their faces, and thy forehead strong against their foreheads.", insight:"Face against face, forehead against forehead; God hardens His servants for hard assignments."},
{q:"How hard did God make Ezekiel's forehead?", options:["As an adamant harder than flint","As soft wax","As polished silver","As common stone"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:9",verse:"As an adamant harder than flint have I made thy forehead: fear them not, neither be dismayed at their looks, though they be a rebellious house.", insight:"Adamant harder than flint; God gives backbone to match the burden."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel to do with God's words?", options:["Receive in thine heart, and hear with thine ears","Write them on the temple wall","Teach them to the children first","Hide them from the people"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:10",verse:"Moreover he said unto me, Son of man, all my words that I shall speak unto thee receive in thine heart, and hear with thine ears.", insight:"Receive in the heart, hear with the ears; the word must dwell in us before it works through us."},
{q:"To whom in the captivity was Ezekiel sent?", options:["To them of the captivity, unto the children of thy people","To the king's counselors","To the Babylonian priests","To the merchants of Tel-abib"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:11",verse:"And go, get thee to them of the captivity, unto the children of thy people, and speak unto them, and tell them, Thus saith the Lord GOD; whether they will hear, or whether they will forbear.", insight:"To the children of thy people; God sends us to our own first."},
{q:"What did the Spirit do with Ezekiel?", options:["The spirit took me up","The spirit made him dumb","The spirit sent him to Babylon","The spirit hid him in a cave"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:12",verse:"Then the spirit took me up, and I heard behind me a voice of a great rushing, saying, Blessed be the glory of the LORD from his place.", insight:"The Spirit took him up; those who eat the word are lifted by the Spirit."},
{q:"What did Ezekiel hear behind him?", options:["A voice of a great rushing, saying, Blessed be the glory of the LORD from his place","The weeping of the captives","The marching of armies","The silence of the desert"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:12",verse:"Then the spirit took me up, and I heard behind me a voice of a great rushing, saying, Blessed be the glory of the LORD from his place.", insight:"Blessed be the glory of the LORD; even in judgment, glory is praised."},
{q:"In what spirit did Ezekiel go with the Spirit?", options:["In bitterness, in the heat of my spirit; but the hand of the LORD was strong upon me","In joy and singing","In fear and trembling","In peace and quietness"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:14",verse:"So the spirit lifted me up, and took me away, and I went in bitterness, in the heat of my spirit; but the hand of the LORD was strong upon me.", insight:"Bitterness in his spirit, yet the hand of the LORD was strong upon him; God carries us through our heaviness."},
{q:"Where did Ezekiel sit among the captives?", options:["At Tel-abib, by the river of Chebar","At the gates of Babylon","In the plains of Dura","By the walls of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:15",verse:"Then I came to them of the captivity at Tel-abib, that dwelt by the river of Chebar, and I sat where they sat, and remained there astonished among them seven days.", insight:"He sat where they sat; the prophet shares the place of the people he serves."},
{q:"How long did Ezekiel sit astonished among them?", options:["Seven days","Three days","Forty days","One full year"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:15",verse:"Then I came to them of the captivity at Tel-abib, that dwelt by the river of Chebar, and I sat where they sat, and remained there astonished among them seven days.", insight:"Seven days astonished; sometimes the wisest beginning is silent sitting with the suffering."},
{q:"What office did God give Ezekiel?", options:["A watchman unto the house of Israel","A king over the captives","A priest of the temple","A scribe of the law"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:17",verse:"Son of man, I have made thee a watchman unto the house of Israel: therefore hear the word at my mouth, and give them warning from me.", insight:"A watchman; God posts His servants on the wall, accountable for the warning."},
{q:"What happens if the watchman fails to warn the wicked?", options:["The wicked dies in his iniquity, but his blood God requires at the watchman's hand","The wicked is spared","The watchman is forgiven","Nothing happens to either"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:18",verse:"When I say unto the wicked, Thou shalt surely die; and thou givest him not warning, nor speakest to warn the wicked from his wicked way, to save his life; the same wicked man shall die in his iniquity; but his blood will I require at thine hand.", insight:"His blood will I require at thine hand; the unspoken warning is a debt we owe."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel told to do after seeing the glory in the plain?", options:["Go, shut thyself within thine house","Flee to the mountains","Return to the river","Gather the elders"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 3:24",verse:"Then the spirit entered into me, and set me upon my feet, and spake with me, and said unto me, Go, shut thyself within thine house.", insight:"Shut thyself within thine house; God sometimes seasons His prophets in solitude."}
]},
{n:4, name:"The Siege Sign", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 4:6",verse:"And when thou hast accomplished them, lie again on thy right side, and thou shalt bear the iniquity of the house of Judah forty days: I have appointed thee each day for a year."}, facts:["Did you know God appointed Ezekiel 'each day for a year,' so his 390 days on one side stood for 390 years of Israel's iniquity (Ezekiel 4:6)?","Did you know Ezekiel's siege bread mixed six grains in one vessel: wheat, barley, beans, lentiles, millet, and fitches (Ezekiel 4:9)?","Did you know Ezekiel's daily ration during the siege sign was weighed out: twenty shekels of bread a day, and water measured to the sixth part of a hin (Ezekiel 4:10)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Ezekiel told to take and portray?", options:["A tile, and pourtray upon it the city, even Jerusalem","A scroll, and write the law","A stone, and carve the commandments","A board, and paint the temple"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:1",verse:"Thou also, son of man, take thee a tile, and lay it before thee, and pourtray upon it the city, even Jerusalem:", insight:"A tile for Jerusalem; God turns a prophet's art into a sermon."},
{q:"What warlike things were set against the pictured city?", options:["A fort, a mount, a camp, and battering rams round about","Catapults and towers of brass","Ships and chariots","Swords and shields only"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:2",verse:"And lay siege against it, and build a fort against it, and cast a mount against it; set the camp also against it, and set battering rams against it round about.", insight:"Fort, mount, camp, and rams; the sign showed judgment in full battle array."},
{q:"What did the iron pan represent in the siege sign?", options:["A wall of iron between thee and the city","A shield for the prophet","A cooking vessel for the siege","A mirror of God's face"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:3",verse:"Moreover take thou unto thee an iron pan, and set it for a wall of iron between thee and the city: and set thy face against it, and it shall be besieged, and thou shalt lay siege against it. This shall be a sign to the house of Israel.", insight:"A wall of iron; sin builds a wall between God and His people."},
{q:"What was the acted-out siege to be?", options:["A sign to the house of Israel","A game for the children","A warning to Babylon","A memorial of the exodus"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:3",verse:"Moreover take thou unto thee an iron pan, and set it for a wall of iron between thee and the city: and set thy face against it, and it shall be besieged, and thou shalt lay siege against it. This shall be a sign to the house of Israel.", insight:"A sign to the house of Israel; acted parables preach to eyes as well as ears."},
{q:"On which side was Ezekiel to lie first?", options:["Upon his left side","Upon his right side","Upon his back","Upon his face"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:4",verse:"Lie thou also upon thy left side, and lay the iniquity of the house of Israel upon it: according to the number of the days that thou shalt lie upon it thou shalt bear their iniquity.", insight:"The left side first; every detail of obedience matters to God."},
{q:"Whose iniquity was laid on Ezekiel as he lay?", options:["The iniquity of the house of Israel","The iniquity of Babylon","The iniquity of the priests","His own iniquity"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:4",verse:"Lie thou also upon thy left side, and lay the iniquity of the house of Israel upon it: according to the number of the days that thou shalt lie upon it thou shalt bear their iniquity.", insight:"He bore their iniquity in symbol; the true Bearer would come in flesh."},
{q:"How many days was Ezekiel to bear Israel's iniquity?", options:["Three hundred and ninety days","Forty days","Seventy days","One hundred days"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:5",verse:"For I have laid upon thee the years of their iniquity, according to the number of the days, three hundred and ninety days: so shalt thou bear the iniquity of the house of Israel.", insight:"Three hundred and ninety days; God measures iniquity in years, and mercy in days of warning."},
{q:"What did the days of lying on his side represent?", options:["The years of their iniquity, according to the number of the days","The years of the captivity","The days of creation","The reigns of the kings"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:5",verse:"For I have laid upon thee the years of their iniquity, according to the number of the days, three hundred and ninety days: so shalt thou bear the iniquity of the house of Israel.", insight:"Years for days; God counts sin by the year and grace by the moment."},
{q:"On which side was Ezekiel to lie for Judah?", options:["On his right side","On his left side","On his back","Upright against the wall"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:6",verse:"And when thou hast accomplished them, lie again on thy right side, and thou shalt bear the iniquity of the house of Judah forty days: I have appointed thee each day for a year.", insight:"The right side for Judah; each house of God's people answers for its own sins."},
{q:"What did each day of Ezekiel's lying represent?", options:["Each day for a year","Each day for a month","Each day for a lifetime","Each day for a jubilee"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:6",verse:"And when thou hast accomplished them, lie again on thy right side, and thou shalt bear the iniquity of the house of Judah forty days: I have appointed thee each day for a year.", insight:"A day for a year; God's arithmetic turns symbols into history."},
{q:"Toward what was Ezekiel to set his face?", options:["Toward the siege of Jerusalem, with his arm uncovered","Toward Babylon, with his head covered","Toward the river, with his hands lifted","Toward the temple, with his face veiled"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:7",verse:"Therefore thou shalt set thy face toward the siege of Jerusalem, and thine arm shall be uncovered, and thou shalt prophesy against it.", insight:"Face toward the siege, arm uncovered; prophecy confronts, it does not hide."},
{q:"What kept Ezekiel from turning during the siege sign?", options:["Bands laid upon him","A wall of stone","His own resolve","The people's hands"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:8",verse:"And, behold, I will lay bands upon thee, and thou shalt not turn thee from one side to another, till thou hast ended the days of thy siege.", insight:"Bands upon him; God's call can hold us steadier than our own strength."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel to mix for his siege bread?", options:["Wheat, barley, beans, lentiles, millet, and fitches","Only fine wheat flour","Barley and honey","Manna and quails"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:9",verse:"Take thou also unto thee wheat, and barley, and beans, and lentiles, and millet, and fitches, and put them in one vessel, and make thee bread thereof, according to the number of the days that thou shalt lie upon thy side, three hundred and ninety days shalt thou eat thereof.", insight:"Six grains in one vessel; siege bread is the food of judgment."},
{q:"How long was he to eat the mixed bread?", options:["Three hundred and ninety days","Forty days","Seven days","Until the siege ended, uncounted"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:9",verse:"Take thou also unto thee wheat, and barley, and beans, and lentiles, and millet, and fitches, and put them in one vessel, and make thee bread thereof, according to the number of the days that thou shalt lie upon thy side, three hundred and ninety days shalt thou eat thereof.", insight:"For all the days of his lying; judgment has a set number, and mercy sets the table."},
{q:"How much bread was Ezekiel to eat each day?", options:["Twenty shekels, by weight","A full loaf","Ten shekels","As much as he desired"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:10",verse:"And thy meat which thou shalt eat shall be by weight, twenty shekels a day: from time to time shalt thou eat it.", insight:"Weighed out; in judgment, even bread is counted."},
{q:"How much water was Ezekiel to drink?", options:["The sixth part of an hin, by measure","A full hin","A cup at morning","Water without measure"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:11",verse:"Thou shalt drink also water by measure, the sixth part of an hin: from time to time shalt thou drink.", insight:"Measured water; God marks the portion of affliction exactly."},
{q:"How was the siege bread to be baked?", options:["As barley cakes, baked with dung in their sight","In a hot oven with oil","Under ashes of cedar wood","On the iron pan"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:12",verse:"And thou shalt eat it as barley cakes, and thou shalt bake it with dung that cometh out of man, in their sight.", insight:"Baked with dung in their sight; defilement was part of the message."},
{q:"What did Ezekiel plead about his own life?", options:["His soul had not been polluted, nor had abominable flesh entered his mouth","He was too old for the sign","He had no tile to use","The people would not watch"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:14",verse:"Then said I, Ah Lord GOD! behold, my soul hath not been polluted: for from my youth up even till now have I not eaten of that which dieth of itself, or is torn in pieces; neither came there abominable flesh into my mouth.", insight:"'Ah Lord GOD'; even in obedience, the prophet may plead, and God hears."},
{q:"What merciful change did God grant?", options:["Cow's dung for man's dung","Clean wood for the fire","An oven instead of open flame","Bread without baking"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:15",verse:"Then he said unto me, Lo, I have given thee cow’s dung for man’s dung, and thou shalt prepare thy bread therewith.", insight:"Cow's dung for man's dung; God tempers the sign without cancelling the message."},
{q:"What did breaking the staff of bread mean for Jerusalem?", options:["They would eat bread by weight with care, and drink water by measure with astonishment","They would feast in the temple","They would find manna again","They would eat only herbs"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 4:16",verse:"Moreover he said unto me, Son of man, behold, I will break the staff of bread in Jerusalem: and they shall eat bread by weight, and with care; and they shall drink water by measure, and with astonishment:", insight:"Bread by weight, water by measure; when God breaks the staff of bread, every crumb is counted."}
]},
{n:5, name:"Burned, Struck, Scattered", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 5:13",verse:"Thus shall mine anger be accomplished, and I will cause my fury to rest upon them, and I will be comforted: and they shall know that I the LORD have spoken it in my zeal, when I have accomplished my fury in them."}, facts:["Did you know the hair Ezekiel shaved and divided stood for Jerusalem's people: a third burned, a third struck by the sword, a third scattered to the wind (Ezekiel 5:12)?","Did you know God said He would diminish Jerusalem because she had defiled His sanctuary with detestable things, and His eye would not spare (Ezekiel 5:11)?","Did you know Jerusalem's judgment would make her 'a reproach and a taunt, an instruction and an astonishment' to the surrounding nations (Ezekiel 5:15)?"], questions:[
{q:"What instrument was Ezekiel told to take?", options:["A sharp knife, a barber's razor","A sword of battle","A sickle of harvest","A chisel of stone"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:1",verse:"And thou, son of man, take thee a sharp knife, take thee a barber’s razor, and cause it to pass upon thine head and upon thy beard: then take thee balances to weigh, and divide the hair.", insight:"A barber's razor for a prophet; God's signs use the ordinary to declare the terrible."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel to do with the razor and the hair?", options:["Cause it to pass upon his head and beard, then weigh and divide the hair in balances","Shave the heads of the elders","Cut the hair of the captives","Trim the wicks of the lamps"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:1",verse:"And thou, son of man, take thee a sharp knife, take thee a barber’s razor, and cause it to pass upon thine head and upon thy beard: then take thee balances to weigh, and divide the hair.", insight:"His own head and beard; the prophet bears the sign in his own flesh."},
{q:"What were the three fates of the divided hair?", options:["A third burned with fire in the midst of the city, a third smitten with the knife, a third scattered in the wind","All three burned outside the camp","All three buried in the earth","All three given to the priests"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:2",verse:"Thou shalt burn with fire a third part in the midst of the city, when the days of the siege are fulfilled: and thou shalt take a third part, and smite about it with a knife: and a third part thou shalt scatter in the wind; and I will draw out a sword after them.", insight:"Burned, smitten, scattered; judgment comes in measured thirds."},
{q:"What was to be done with a few of the hairs?", options:["Take a few in number, and bind them in thy skirts","Weave them into a cord","Cast them into the river","Give them to the children"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:3",verse:"Thou shalt also take thereof a few in number, and bind them in thy skirts.", insight:"A few bound in his skirts; even in judgment, God keeps a remnant close."},
{q:"What was to happen to some of the bound hairs?", options:["Cast them into the midst of the fire, and burn them","Set them free","Hide them in the temple","Send them to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:4",verse:"Then take of them again, and cast them into the midst of the fire, and burn them in the fire; for thereof shall a fire come forth into all the house of Israel.", insight:"Even some of the remnant pass through fire; being kept does not always mean being spared."},
{q:"How does God describe Jerusalem's place?", options:["This is Jerusalem, set in the midst of the nations","A city hidden in the hills","The least of all cities","A city of the Chaldeans"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:5",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; This is Jerusalem: I have set it in the midst of the nations and countries that are round about her.", insight:"Set in the midst of the nations; privilege of place increases the weight of sin."},
{q:"What had Jerusalem done with God's judgments?", options:["Changed them into wickedness more than the nations","Kept them more faithfully than any","Written them on the gates","Taught them to the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:6",verse:"And she hath changed my judgments into wickedness more than the nations, and my statutes more than the countries that are round about her: for they have refused my judgments and my statutes, they have not walked in them.", insight:"Changed judgments into wickedness; the worst sin is perverting the greatest light."},
{q:"How had Jerusalem outdone the nations?", options:["Multiplied more than the nations round about, yet kept neither statutes nor judgments","Built more temples","Kept more feasts","Honored more kings"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:7",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Because ye multiplied more than the nations that are round about you, and have not walked in my statutes, neither have kept my judgments, neither have done according to the judgments of the nations that are round about you;", insight:"Multiplied in sin beyond the nations; abundance without obedience is the heaviest guilt."},
{q:"What terrifying words did God speak against the city?", options:["Behold, I, even I, am against thee","I will be thy shield","I have forgotten thy sins","I will dwell among thee in peace"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:8",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I, even I, am against thee, and will execute judgments in the midst of thee in the sight of the nations.", insight:"'I, even I, am against thee'; there is no darker sentence than God against us."},
{q:"How unprecedented would the judgment be?", options:["What God had not done, nor would do again the like","A common famine","A brief siege","A mild rebuke"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:9",verse:"And I will do in thee that which I have not done, and whereunto I will not do any more the like, because of all thine abominations.", insight:"Never done, never to be done again; some judgments are unique in history."},
{q:"What horror would the siege bring?", options:["Fathers would eat sons, and sons would eat fathers","The walls would fall in a day","The temple would be moved","The river would dry up"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:10",verse:"Therefore the fathers shall eat the sons in the midst of thee, and the sons shall eat their fathers; and I will execute judgments in thee, and the whole remnant of thee will I scatter into all the winds.", insight:"Fathers eating sons; sin's harvest is the ruin of the dearest bonds."},
{q:"What would become of the remnant?", options:["The whole remnant scattered into all the winds","The remnant gathered in peace","The remnant crowned with honor","The remnant hidden in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:10",verse:"Therefore the fathers shall eat the sons in the midst of thee, and the sons shall eat their fathers; and I will execute judgments in thee, and the whole remnant of thee will I scatter into all the winds.", insight:"Scattered into all the winds; judgment disperses what pride had gathered."},
{q:"Why would God's eye not spare Jerusalem?", options:["She had defiled His sanctuary with detestable things and abominations","She had forgotten the feasts","She had taxed the poor","She had allied with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:11",verse:"Wherefore, as I live, saith the Lord GOD; Surely, because thou hast defiled my sanctuary with all thy detestable things, and with all thine abominations, therefore will I also diminish thee; neither shall mine eye spare, neither will I have any pity.", insight:"Defiled sanctuary, no pity; God guards the honor of His house."},
{q:"What threefold fate awaited the people?", options:["A third pestilence and famine, a third the sword, a third scattered to the winds","All would be enslaved","All would be pardoned","Two thirds would flee"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:12",verse:"A third part of thee shall die with the pestilence, and with famine shall they be consumed in the midst of thee: and a third part shall fall by the sword round about thee and I will scatter a third part into all the winds, and I will draw out a sword after them.", insight:"Pestilence, sword, scattering; God's judgments march in solemn order."},
{q:"What would follow those scattered to the winds?", options:["A sword drawn out after them","A swift return home","A land of plenty","A new covenant at once"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:12",verse:"A third part of thee shall die with the pestilence, and with famine shall they be consumed in the midst of thee: and a third part shall fall by the sword round about thee and I will scatter a third part into all the winds, and I will draw out a sword after them.", insight:"A sword after them; there is no outrunning the judgment of God."},
{q:"When would God's anger be accomplished?", options:["When He caused His fury to rest upon them","When the temple fell","When Babylon repented","Never, for He would relent"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:13",verse:"Thus shall mine anger be accomplished, and I will cause my fury to rest upon them, and I will be comforted: and they shall know that I the LORD have spoken it in my zeal, when I have accomplished my fury in them.", insight:"His fury would rest; judgment has an end, and God's zeal is satisfied in truth."},
{q:"What would Jerusalem become?", options:["Waste, and a reproach among the nations round about","A praise in all the earth","A garden of delight","A city of refuge"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:14",verse:"Moreover I will make thee waste, and a reproach among the nations that are round about thee, in the sight of all that pass by.", insight:"Waste and a reproach; sin makes a spectacle of the sinner."},
{q:"What would Jerusalem be to the surrounding nations?", options:["A reproach and a taunt, an instruction and an astonishment","An example of blessing","A pattern of worship","A wonder of wealth"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:15",verse:"So it shall be a reproach and a taunt, an instruction and an astonishment unto the nations that are round about thee, when I shall execute judgments in thee in anger and in fury and in furious rebukes. I the LORD have spoken it.", insight:"Instruction and astonishment; God teaches the nations through the judgment of His own."},
{q:"What are called the evil arrows of famine?", options:["Sent for their destruction, to break the staff of bread","Blessings in disguise","Warnings only","Dreams of the night"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:16",verse:"When I shall send upon them the evil arrows of famine, which shall be for their destruction, and which I will send to destroy you: and I will increase the famine upon you, and will break your staff of bread:", insight:"Evil arrows of famine; hunger itself becomes God's artillery."},
{q:"What fourfold judgment would come upon them?", options:["Famine and evil beasts, pestilence and blood, and the sword","Rain, hail, snow, and wind","War, peace, famine, plenty","Lions, bears, wolves, and serpents"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 5:17",verse:"So will I send upon you famine and evil beasts, and they shall bereave thee; and pestilence and blood shall pass through thee; and I will bring the sword upon thee. I the LORD have spoken it.", insight:"Famine, beasts, pestilence, sword; the full quiver of judgment, and still 'I the LORD have spoken it.'"}
]}
]);
